        "title": "Whistling in La Gomera",
        "text": "An ancient language of whistles, which allowed people to communicate over long distances long before the mobile phone was invented, is to be saved from extinction on a volcanic island off the west coast of Africa. The island is one of the Canary Islands and is called La Gomera. The language, Silbo Gomero, which sounds like birdsong, was used by the islanders to communicate up to three kilometres across the deep valleys that spread out from La Gomera's central volcanic peak.\n\nWhen the island was opened up to tourism and the telephone arrived, Silbo Gomero began to die out. Fortunately, the island authorities realised what they were losing before it was too late, and since 1999 Silbo Gomero has been part of the school curriculum for children up to the age of 14. About 3,000 students spend 25 minutes a week learning it, which is enough to understand the basics. The name Silbo Gomero comes from the Spanish verb silbar, meaning 'to whistle', and Gomero, meaning 'from the island of La Gomera'.\n\nThe language is made up of four vowels and four consonants, which can be whistled to make more than 4,000 words. In the past, children learnt it from their parents, but as fewer and fewer adults taught it to their children, the government had to take over. According to Eugenio Darias, a teacher of Silbo Gomero and director of the island's Silbo programme, 'So far there are few really good silbadores – fluent whistlers of the language – but lots of students are learning to use it and understand it. We've been very pleased with the results.'\n\nAn important step towards saving the language was the first International Congress of Whistled Languages, which was held in La Gomera in 2003. Whistling like Silbo has been found in parts of Greece, Turkey, China and Mexico, but none of it is as developed as Silbo Gomero. Research will now be carried out in Venezuela, Cuba and Texas – all places to which Gomerans have traditionally emigrated, and where traces of the language still survive.\n\nDr Francisco Rivero is a researcher at La Laguna University in Santa Cruz de Tenerife. 'Historically, from the earliest settlers until quite recently, Silbo Gomero was the mobile phone of its day. It allowed people to communicate across great distances because its frequency allowed the sound to carry.' Although Silbo probably began in the Atlas Mountains of North Africa 2,500 years ago, it was adapted to La Gomera by taking on Spanish speech patterns. 'It relies on vowels rather than consonants,' explains Dr Rivero. 'These are whistled at different frequencies, using Spanish grammar. If we spoke English, we'd use an English structure for whistling. It's not just disconnected words – it flows, and you can quite easily have a proper conversation with someone.'\n\n'Silbo Gomero is the most important pre-Hispanic cultural heritage we have. It is unique and has many kinds of value – historical, linguistic, anthropological and aesthetic,' says Moisés Plasencia, Director of the Canary Islands' Historical Heritage department. Señor Plasencia has begun working to persuade UNESCO to support La Gomera's efforts to save the island's language."
